 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB across both queues. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Replacement is 2Q: two queues divide the 12 GiB budget, separating first-use sheets from reused sheets so a sequential screening cannot evict a comparison set.
+
+- **A1 (new queue)** — sheets enter A1 on first admission, managed FIFO. Eviction from A1 takes the oldest sheet, so a sequential scan only displaces other one-use sheets.
+- **A2 (reuse queue)** — when a request hits a sheet in A1, the sheet is promoted to A2's most-recently-used end; A2 is managed LRU, so a hit in A2 refreshes position.
+- **Admission path** — a completed render is admitted to A1 even if the same key was evicted earlier; no ghost list records prior evictions. A sheet must be referenced while resident in A1 to reach A2.
+- **Eviction order** — when admitting a sheet would exceed the bound: if A1 occupies more than its share of the budget, evict the A1 tail; otherwise evict the least-recently-used sheet from A2.
+- **A1 share** — the fraction of the 12 GiB budget reserved for A1 before A2 is tapped, initially 25%.
+- **Reference definition** — only completed admissions and cache hits count as references. Requests that share a pending render job do not count as reuse; each key's first completed admission places it in A1.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q protects the reused comparison set during long sequential screenings, where LRU would evict it, and adapts immediately when editors switch projects, where aged frequency counters would retain yesterday's favorites. It costs two queues and a size split to tune, but all changes stay inside the preview worker; the external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget are unchanged. |
+| 2Q variant | Simplified two-queue (A1 FIFO + A2 LRU, no ghost list) | Full 2Q with an A1out ghost list | The ghost list adds a third structure to give re-referenced evictees a direct path to A2. Without it a re-requested sheet re-enters A1 and needs one more hit to promote — the common case still lands in A2. The extra bookkeeping is deferred until pilot hit rates justify it. |
+| A1 share | 25% of the 12 GiB budget | Larger (scan-tolerant) or smaller (reuse-heavy) splits | Starting value within the usual 25–50% band; tuned during the pilot against observed workload mix. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q, chosen to protect comparison sheets during big screenings at the cost of extra bookkeeping; all policy changes confined to the preview worker.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Tune the A1 share (initially 25%) against pilot hit rates; adjust by production workload mix.
+2. Add the A1out ghost list if pilot data shows sheets frequently re-requested shortly after A1 eviction.
